def str55 : String := "{\"allowed_variation\":{\"grounding\":[{\"end_line\":938,\"path\":\"standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md\",\"quote\":\"### PC-04 — Recursive authority and information eligibility\\n\\nA permitted A → B use does not entail A's access to B's dependencies. Neither\\nA → B plus B → C nor two routes to a shared leaf imply a union of their grants.\\nEach onward acquisition, disclosure or delegated effect needs authority for that\\nactor, purpose and path, including any permitted onward use of returned material.\\nExisting delegation may authorize a chain explicitly; its scope cannot expand\\nsilently at a recursive step.\\n\\nA provider may encapsulate internal dependencies behind an adequate contract.\\nConsumers need not flatten every implementation dependency into a direct edge.\\nWhere an outer claim materially relies on a far-side contract, assumption,\\nevidence item or acquisition, that reliance and its applicable authority must be\\npreserved through the boundary. Unknown impact is not evidence of encapsulation.\\n\\nDurable Product consumption and bounded authorized inspection are different\\nrelations. A Reviewer or diagnostic Worker may inspect a larger causal cone under\\nan explicit access grant without thereby adopting those internals as a runtime\\ndependency or gaining a write grant. If findings become a continuing construction\\nconstraint or dependency, their producer, applicability and governing relation\\nmust be retained.\",\"section\":\"repo://stdo-v3.0.2-source/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#pc-04--recursive-authority-and-information-eligibility\",\"start_line\":917}],\"statements\":[\"Encapsulate internal dependencies behind an adequate provider contract; authorize broader causal-cone inspection separately.\",\"Choose an explicit direct-edge inventory for public constituent contracts even where an adequate provider contract could encapsulate them, with each target, purpose and grant separately established.\"]},\"applies_to\":[\"urn:a-c:generation:630ce49ea536c4b3949b529c:record:9cf5abaccc2c192f2b63905987f18afc\"],\"basis\":\"urn:a-c:generation:630ce49ea536c4b3949b529c:basis\",\"context\":\"urn:a-c:generation:630ce49ea536c4b3949b529c:context:source-first-proposal\",\"forbidden_variation\":{\"grounding\":[{\"end_line\":938,\"path\":\"standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md\",\"quote\":\"### PC-04 — Recursive authority and information eligibility\\n\\nA permitted A → B use does not entail A's access to B's dependencies. Neither\\nA → B plus B → C nor two routes to a shared leaf imply a union of their grants.\\nEach onward acquisition, disclosure or delegated effect needs authority for that\\nactor, purpose and path, including any permitted onward use of returned material.\\nExisting delegation may authorize a chain explicitly; its scope cannot expand\\nsilently at a recursive step.\\n\\nA provider may encapsulate internal dependencies behind an adequate contract.\\nConsumers need not flatten every implementation dependency into a direct edge.\\nWhere an outer claim materially relies on a far-side contract, assumption,\\nevidence item or acquisition, that reliance and its applicable authority must be\\npreserved through the boundary. Unknown impact is not evidence of encapsulation.\\n\\nDurable Product consumption and bounded authorized inspection are different\\nrelations. A Reviewer or diagnostic Worker may inspect a larger causal cone under\\nan explicit access grant without thereby adopting those internals as a runtime\\ndependency or gaining a write grant. If findings become a continuing construction\\nconstraint or dependency, their producer, applicability and governing relation\\nmust be retained.\",\"section\":\"repo://stdo-v3.0.2-source/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#pc-04--recursive-authority-and-information-eligibility\",\"start_line\":917}],\"statements\":[\"Infer or union grants through recursive reachability, or omit material far-side reliance and its applicable authority.\"]},\"id\":\"urn:a-c:generation:63